---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';

const path = '/compare/activecampaign-vs-brevo';
const title = pageTitle(site, path.slice(1));
const desc = description(site, path.slice(1));
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which setup should we evaluate first?",
    "answer": "Evaluate ActiveCampaign when a cart needs an accountable sales or account handoff. Evaluate Brevo PushOwl when Shopify campaigns and store-connected messaging are the main job. Demonstrate the required workflow in each; neither is an automatic winner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Brevo only support broadcasts?",
    "answer": "No. Its Shopify guide documents customer, product and order sync, onsite tracking and automations including abandonment. Verify your account mapping and the events used by the proposed flow."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can both handle guest checkout?",
    "answer": "An email address alone does not establish recovery eligibility. ActiveCampaign documents a Shopify checkout-stage requirement; in either setup verify identity, permission, event arrival and the return link with a fresh guest checkout."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is purchaser suppression identical?",
    "answer": "Use the same acceptance requirement, not an assumption of identical behavior: a purchase must prevent the next recovery message, including one already queued. Demonstrate it in the installed setup."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which is cheaper to operate?",
    "answer": "Quote the required connection, contacts, sends, seats, channels and implementation work. Record the time your operator needs to change and validate a flow; no universal maintenance advantage is established here."
  },
  {
    "question": "What is the first automation to rebuild post-migration?",
    "answer": "Choose by dependency. If welcome owns the shared incentive or contact eligibility, validate it before cart recovery. If cart recovery is independent and its purchase exits pass, it can move first. Reconcile each queue and pause the old sender before enabling its replacement."
  },
  {
    "question": "How long should a fair trial run?",
    "answer": "First pass the event and queue acceptance cases. Then choose any holdout allocation and observation window from eligible traffic, purchase delay and the contribution difference worth detecting. Fourteen days and five percent are not universal requirements."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container max-w-5xl">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <header class="max-w-4xl mt-6">
        <p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head · Shopify cart recovery</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">ActiveCampaign vs Brevo for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">This comparison is about a specific operating decision: should a Shopify store use ActiveCampaign’s automation and CRM context, or Brevo PushOwl’s Shopify campaign and messaging connection? The answer changes with order value, margin, sales involvement, and how much event governance the team can maintain.</p>
        <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">Use this proposed scorecard for cart timing, purchaser suppression, incentive control, Shopify data, reporting, and the time required to change a flow before a promotion. The same workflow should be tested in both tools; platform-attributed recovery alone is not evidence of incremental revenue.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-10 card overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>ActiveCampaign</th><th>Brevo</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Best fit</td><td>CRM-linked lifecycle and complex branches</td><td>Practical campaigns and broad sending needs</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cart recovery</td><td>Conditional branches, tags, and sales context</td><td>Clear campaigns and automation for common paths</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shopify evaluation</td><td>Event mapping and customer context</td><td>Event availability and timing must be verified</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pricing lens</td><td>Contacts, plan tier, seats, and features</td><td>Send volume, contacts, and channel features</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operational risk</td><td>Demonstrate account ownership and branch maintenance</td><td>Demonstrate the required handoff and campaign changes</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Start with the handoff the store actually needs</h2>
        <p>A wholesale buyer with an open deal may need a representative rather than a consumer code. A returning DTC buyer may need reassurance without an incentive. ActiveCampaign is worth evaluating when these are different owned journeys: require a cart event to select the right contact, account and deal, create one assigned task, and record whether the representative acted before another reminder is sent. CRM context earns its place only if someone uses the handoff.</p>
        <p>For a campaign-led store, evaluate whether Brevo PushOwl lets the campaign operator maintain recovery alongside broadcasts and other store messages. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014912879-Brevo-PushOwl-app-for-Shopify-Connect-your-Shopify-store-with-Brevo-PushOwl">current Shopify guide</a> documents customer, product and order sync, onsite tracking and abandonment automations. This is more than a contact import. A simple reminder is not evidence that the store needs a deal pipeline; an existing Brevo account still needs the correct store connection.</p>
        <p>Compare the actual editing work: ask the assigned operator to change a branch, revise a campaign exclusion and pause a queued send. Record access required, manual steps and elapsed operator time. Treat operating burden as an outcome of that exercise, not an inherent advantage of either brand.</p>

        <h2>Establish the event boundary before comparing branches</h2>
        <p><a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/360001045964-Abandoned-cart-overview">ActiveCampaign’s abandoned-cart documentation</a> describes a store/product/category/value trigger and a product content block with a return link. It also specifies a Shopify checkout-stage requirement for synced abandoned-cart data. A known email or product-page visit alone does not prove that event exists. Separate the integration wait from the automation delay when checking delivery timing.</p>
        <p>For Brevo PushOwl, demonstrate the current tracker and store connection with a fresh checkout rather than treating historical customer sync as live abandonment. In both candidates, inspect event time, customer identity, basket lines, currency and destination URL. Repeat as a guest, an existing customer and a customer who changes their basket before the reminder. Identity, permission and event eligibility are separate checks.</p>
        <p>Keep Shopify’s working recovery as the baseline. <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/migrate-abandoned-checkout">Shopify documents abandoned-checkout automation in Messaging</a>, including channel restrictions and recipient settings. Inventory that sender before adding another one. A replacement should solve a demonstrated handoff or campaign requirement while preserving the eligible recovery already in place.</p>

        <h2>One acceptance matrix, with different reasons to pass</h2>
        <p>These are proposed acceptance cases, not observed vendor results or promises of identical suppression. Set the allowable event delay from the next scheduled touch and the store’s service commitment; record event receipt, queue state and actual inbox arrival.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Fixture</th><th>Required observation</th><th>Decision it resolves</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Wholesale contact with an open deal</td><td>One assigned rep task; consumer incentive excluded; rep action changes the next step</td><td>Whether CRM context justifies the ActiveCampaign workflow</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Returning DTC buyer in a campaign audience</td><td>Correct incentive branch and broadcast exclusion after an operator edits the campaign</td><td>Whether the campaign team can own the Brevo workflow without extra reconciliation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Purchase while a reminder is queued</td><td>The next recovery send is cancelled or excluded before delivery</td><td>Whether purchase exits work across the actual queue boundary</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Refund, sold-out variant or changed basket</td><td>Apply the agreed refund policy; pause unavailable-product urgency; use current basket content</td><td>Whether event fields support the store’s policy rather than stale copy</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Open support ticket and service caller</td><td>The service tool’s caller or webhook supplies a usable status; an owner pauses recovery and records resumption</td><td>Whether a service handoff is feasible, including any external integration</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Replayed event and two recovery senders</td><td>One eligible enrollment and one queue owner per channel; no duplicate reminder</td><td>Whether cutover can preserve recovery without overlapping sends</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>If SMS is in scope, separately demonstrate permission, quiet hours, sender coverage, message fees and cross-channel exclusions. Do not infer coordination from the presence of both channel buttons. A contact import, a rep task or a service webhook is not proof that an already queued email was cancelled.</p>

        <h2>Keep the incentive decision separate from the platform decision</h2>
        <p>Illustrative $73 basket at 55% gross margin: unchanged goods cost is $32.85 and full-price gross profit is $40.15. A 19% code costs $13.87 and leaves $26.28 gross profit before fulfillment, payment, messaging and returns. Those costs must be subtracted before calling the remainder contribution. The arithmetic establishes no recovery result.</p>
        <p>For 1,000 eligible abandoned baskets, a hypothetical 5% purchase rate is 50 orders. If all used that code, gross profit would be $1,314 before other variable costs. This is not incremental profit: compare against orders in a randomized no-message group where feasible, normalized per eligible shopper over the same observation window.</p>
        <p>At $342 and 39% gross margin, full-price gross profit is $133.38. The same illustrative 19% code costs $64.98 and leaves $68.40 before other variable costs. Use this high-value fixture to compare a rep handoff with reassurance, shipping information and support access. A sales branch needs an available owner; a campaign branch needs the relevant customer and order fields. Neither earns credit merely for issuing a discount.</p>
        <p>Retain meaningful basket variants in that exercise: bundles need intact bundle value, pre-orders need a known shipping promise, gift carts need delivery information, subscription carts need cadence and recurring-price clarity, and international carts need landed-cost information. These are content and event requirements to demonstrate, not claims that either supplier automatically diagnoses the reason for abandonment.</p>

        <h2>Price the demonstrated workflow</h2>
        <p>Use <a class="link" href="https://www.activecampaign.com/pricing">ActiveCampaign pricing</a>, <a class="link" href="https://www.brevo.com/pricing/">Brevo pricing</a> and the <a class="link" href="https://pushowl.com/pricing">PushOwl plan page</a> linked by the Shopify guide to obtain the allowances for your chosen connection. Record contacts, email volume, seats, channels, CRM features and any service integration as separate quote inputs. Confirm which account and plan the store will actually use.</p>
        <p>Model monthly eligible abandonments × planned touches, then add broadcasts and other lifecycle sends. For example, 1,000 eligible abandonments with at most three touches creates an upper bound of 3,000 recovery sends before purchase exits. Add seasonal volume and contact growth, then include setup and the operator time measured in the acceptance exercise. Do not infer a lower total from a starting tier or assume the same billing units.</p>

        <h2>Migrate in dependency order, with one owner per queue</h2>
        <p>Export consent provenance by channel and purpose, opt-outs, tags, recent purchases, account/deal mappings and pending sends. Draw dependencies before choosing the first flow. If welcome owns shared eligibility or the incentive promise, validate it before cart recovery. If cart recovery is independent and its purchase exits pass, it can move first. Win-back can move independently only after its historical purchase and exclusion data are ready. There is no universal welcome-first or cart-first order.</p>
        <p>For each flow, reconcile pending messages and replayed events, pause the old sender, then enable its replacement after seeded inbox and purchase-exit checks. Keep a rollback copy, credentials and a named operator for as long as the agreed observation window requires. Check Shopify’s own queue too. Do not run duplicate live recovery sends as a substitute for parallel validation.</p>
        <p>Re-test affected cases immediately after a checkout, subscription, catalog or market change. A new wholesale channel warrants the account/deal fixture; a new SMS branch warrants permission and collision checks. Schedule review from those dependencies and actual traffic rather than a fixed ninety-day delay.</p>

        <h2>Make the CRM-versus-campaign decision</h2>
        <p>Shortlist ActiveCampaign if the account/deal fixture produces an actionable handoff that your team will maintain. Shortlist Brevo PushOwl if store-connected automation and campaign changes pass with an accountable operator. If both pass, compare total cost and measured editing effort. If neither adds a needed capability over the Shopify baseline, keep that baseline while resolving the missing requirement.</p>
        <p>Only after operational acceptance should you evaluate incremental contribution. Choose holdout size and duration from traffic, purchase delay and the smallest worthwhile contribution difference; report uncertainty when volume is insufficient. A fourteen-day trial or five-percent holdout does not by itself establish a winner.</p>
        <p>Use the <a class="link" href="/guides">guides</a> and <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">abandoned-cart use case</a> for implementation context. The <a class="link" href="/compare">comparison hub</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps">apps index</a> help shortlist alternatives. <a class="link" href="/compare/activecampaign-vs-rejoiner">ActiveCampaign vs Rejoiner</a> addresses internal ownership versus a managed handoff; <a class="link" href="/compare/mailchimp-vs-brevo">Mailchimp vs Brevo</a> addresses audience migration and permission precedence. This page’s deciding case is whether cart recovery needs an owned CRM handoff or a campaign operator.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="ActiveCampaign vs Brevo FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
